{
  "title": "React 29: Testing React",
  "subtitle": "React Explained",
  "audio": null,
  "beats": [
    {
      "id": "pyramid",
      "min": 9,
      "duration": 14.0,
      "text": "What to test, and where.",
      "visual": "chips",
      "items": [
        [
          "Unit: hooks and utilities",
          "blue"
        ],
        [
          "Component: behavior with RTL",
          "green"
        ],
        [
          "Integration: flows and routing",
          "yellow"
        ],
        [
          "E2E: a few critical paths",
          "pink"
        ]
      ],
      "say": "Test at several levels. Unit tests for hooks and utility functions, component tests for behavior, integration tests for flows across components and routes, and a small number of end-to-end tests for the critical journeys.",
      "voice": "/audio/react-29-testing/pyramid.wav"
    },
    {
      "id": "philosophy",
      "min": 9,
      "duration": 13.5,
      "text": "Test behavior, not implementation.",
      "visual": "compare",
      "left": {
        "title": "Implementation",
        "color": "red",
        "items": [
          "Check state and internals",
          "Breaks on every refactor"
        ]
      },
      "right": {
        "title": "Behavior",
        "color": "green",
        "items": [
          "What the user sees and does",
          "Survives refactors"
        ]
      },
      "say": "React Testing Library pushes you to test like a user. Don't assert on state or internal functions. Render the component, interact with it, and check what appears on screen. Those tests survive refactoring.",
      "voice": "/audio/react-29-testing/philosophy.wav"
    },
    {
      "id": "rtl",
      "min": 10,
      "duration": 11.5,
      "text": "A component test.",
      "visual": "code",
      "items": [
        [
          "render(<Counter />);",
          "yellow"
        ],
        [
          "",
          "text"
        ],
        [
          "await user.click(",
          "blue"
        ],
        [
          "  screen.getByRole('button', {",
          "blue"
        ],
        [
          "    name: /add/i }));",
          "blue"
        ],
        [
          "",
          "text"
        ],
        [
          "expect(screen.getByText('1'))",
          "green"
        ],
        [
          "  .toBeInTheDocument();",
          "green"
        ]
      ],
      "say": "You render the component, find elements the way a user would, by their role and name, simulate a click, and assert on the result. No knowledge of how the component is built is needed.",
      "voice": "/audio/react-29-testing/rtl.wav"
    },
    {
      "id": "queries",
      "min": 8,
      "duration": 14.5,
      "text": "Choosing queries.",
      "visual": "chips",
      "items": [
        [
          "getByRole first",
          "green"
        ],
        [
          "getByLabelText for forms",
          "blue"
        ],
        [
          "getByText for content",
          "yellow"
        ],
        [
          "data-testid as a last resort",
          "red"
        ]
      ],
      "say": "Prefer queries that mirror how people and screen readers find things: by role first, by label for form fields, and by text for content. Use a test I D only as a last resort. Good queries double as accessibility checks.",
      "voice": "/audio/react-29-testing/queries.wav"
    },
    {
      "id": "network",
      "min": 9,
      "duration": 11.5,
      "text": "Mock the network, not your code.",
      "visual": "code",
      "items": [
        [
          "http.get('/api/user', () =>",
          "yellow"
        ],
        [
          "  HttpResponse.json({ name: 'Ana' })",
          "green"
        ],
        [
          ")",
          "yellow"
        ],
        [
          "",
          "text"
        ],
        [
          "server.listen();  // in test setup",
          "muted"
        ]
      ],
      "say": "For components that fetch data, use Mock Service Worker. It intercepts real requests at the network level, so your components run unchanged, and you control what the server returns.",
      "voice": "/audio/react-29-testing/network.wav"
    },
    {
      "id": "tools",
      "min": 9,
      "duration": 11.5,
      "text": "The toolkit.",
      "visual": "chips",
      "items": [
        [
          "Vitest or Jest as the runner",
          "blue"
        ],
        [
          "Testing Library for components",
          "green"
        ],
        [
          "MSW to mock the network",
          "yellow"
        ],
        [
          "Playwright for end-to-end",
          "pink"
        ]
      ],
      "say": "A typical stack: Vitest or Jest to run tests, React Testing Library for components, Mock Service Worker for the network, and Playwright for end-to-end tests in a real browser.",
      "voice": "/audio/react-29-testing/tools.wav"
    },
    {
      "id": "close",
